            "rationale": "When severely malnourished/deficient patients receive rapid nutritional repletion, cells suddenly begin rapid protein/nucleic acid synthesis, which requires MASSIVE intracellular influx of K+, Mg, and phosphate. These electrolytes shift OUT of serum INTO cells → acute HYPOKALEMIA/HYPOMAGNESEMIA/HYPOPHOSPHATEMIA. This is REFEEDING SYNDROME, a medical emergency. In this patient: rapid RBC production from B12 supplementation caused electrolyte shift. Manifestations: (1) Hypokalemia → muscle weakness, cramps, arrhythmias, cardiac dysfunction, (2) Hypomagnesemia → arrhythmias, anxiety, muscle dysfunction, (3) Hypophosphatemia → rhabdomyolysis, respiratory muscle weakness. Management: (1) STOP rapid B12 dosing temporarily, (2) Aggressive electrolyte replacement: IV potassium chloride, IV magnesium sulfate, IV phosphate, (3) Continuous cardiac monitoring (risk of sudden cardiac death), (4) SLOW B12 repletion (less aggressive dosing), (5) Monitor K/Mg/Phos closely after each dose, (6) ICU admission if severe arrhythmias. This is a MISSED diagnosis frequently — clinicians see B12 treatment as entirely beneficial and don't anticipate refeeding complications."
